Kernel 5 — Action UI Layer (First Production Tooling)
Purpose

Replace devtools/manual action firing with real, minimal UI controls that invoke the same underlying action system.

This kernel is about:

usable controls
role-aware overlays
action triggering from the GUI

It is not about full permission lattice, editor systems, or generalized tooling panels.

Core objective

Make the cave usable without devtools.

A person in the cave should be able to:

speak
react
reveal
hide

through the actual interface.

Assumed current state

Already exists and should not be rebuilt:

auth/session floor
invite/role/access floor
workshop asset upload floor
map GUI
venue routing
cave page
audition hall page
shared real-time cave session
role-filtered illusion layer for first-fire
reveal/hide persistence via action-derived snapshot
Scope

Build the first role-aware control overlay for the-cave.

Required UI controls
Audience

Must have:

reaction controls

Must not have:

reveal/hide controls
actor/director-only controls
Actor

Must have:

speak input
reveal/hide controls if currently allowed by temporary kernel rules
Director

Must have:

speak input
reveal/hide controls
Producer

May inherit director controls for now.

Required behavior
1. Speak from UI

A user with speaking authority can enter speech in the cave UI and trigger the existing perform/speak action.

2. React from UI

Audience can trigger existing reaction actions from the cave UI.

3. Reveal from UI

Authorized role can trigger reveal through the cave UI.

4. Hide from UI

Authorized role can trigger hide through the cave UI.

5. Stage remains role-filtered

The UI controls must sit on top of the existing illusion system, not replace it.

Tooling meaning in this kernel

This kernel establishes the first production tooling, not dev-only tooling.

That means:

real controls
role-aware overlay
same backend action contracts
no separate “debug-only reality”

A button in the UI should call the same action system the devtools call.

UI requirements
Cave layout

Keep current cave structure.

Add only minimal role-aware overlays/controls.

Control overlay

A lightweight interface layer over or alongside the rendered cave view.

Examples:

reveal button
hide button
speak input
reaction strip

Do not overdesign it.

Role gating

UI should differ by role.

This is UI gating only.
Backend enforcement can remain limited in this kernel if needed, but the UI must not expose the wrong controls to the wrong roles.

Temporary authority rule for this kernel

Do not build the full policy system yet.

Use a temporary simple rule:

Director can reveal/hide
Producer can reveal/hide
Actor can reveal/hide only if current temporary implementation chooses to allow it
Audience cannot reveal/hide

If you want the actor to have reveal/hide in Kernel 5 for practical testing, that is acceptable.
Formal policy control comes later.

Non-goals

Do not build in Kernel 5:

full permission lattice
policy editor
per-production policy storage
map work
audition hall improvements
workshop UI
note cards
chat redesign
drag/drop
multi-element tool systems
generalized scene editor
diagnostics dashboard
operator panel
Success criteria

Kernel 5 passes when:

audience can enter the-cave and react using visible UI controls
actor can enter the-cave and speak using visible UI controls
director can reveal/hide fire using visible UI controls
audience never sees reveal/hide controls
role-specific controls differ visibly in the cave UI
all controls use the existing backend action path
reveal/hide still affects audience perception in real time
speech and reactions still work in real time
Builder emphasis

Tell the next agent:

Do not build a tooling framework.
Do not build a panel system.
Do not build policy storage.
Just expose the existing cave actions through minimal role-aware UI.

Recommended implementation stance

Use:

small buttons
one speak input
one reaction strip
one simple control overlay

Do not build:

selection systems
macros
hotkey systems
reusable control libraries

Those come later.

What this kernel should prove

That your system can move from:

hidden backend truth
devtools-driven actions

to:

actual production-facing controls
inside a role-aware venue

That is the first real step from infrastructure into usable software.

